1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,165
One, plus four, plus nine, plus sixteen.

2
00:00:03,165 --> 00:00:04,993
These are the square numbers.

3
00:00:05,393 --> 00:00:08,357
Diya was laying square
tiles at the study table.

4
00:00:08,357 --> 00:00:12,195
A one by one square, then two
by two, then three by three.

5
00:00:12,645 --> 00:00:15,221
How many tiles for the first ten squares?

6
00:00:15,221 --> 00:00:16,648
Or the first hundred?

7
00:00:16,998 --> 00:00:19,046
Adding them one by one is slow.

8
00:00:19,046 --> 00:00:21,667
But one formula does it in a single step.

9
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:24,274
Today: the sum of special series.

10
00:00:24,274 --> 00:00:27,193
Natural numbers, their
squares, and their cubes.

11
00:00:27,843 --> 00:00:29,987
First, a short sign for adding.

12
00:00:29,987 --> 00:00:33,708
The Greek letter sigma, a
big S, means add them all up.

13
00:00:34,108 --> 00:00:39,157
Sigma k, from one to n, means one,
plus two, plus three, all the way to

14
00:00:39,157 --> 00:00:40,057
n.

15
00:00:39,967 --> 00:00:41,836
We already know this one.

16
00:00:41,836 --> 00:00:45,739
The staircase trick gives n
times n plus one, over two.

17
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:47,240
One note.

18
00:00:46,969 --> 00:00:49,572
These sums were dropped
from the current NCERT text.

19
00:00:49,572 --> 00:00:50,717
JEE still asks them.

20
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:52,512
Now the squares.

21
00:00:52,512 --> 00:00:55,466
Watch the tiles grow,
one square at a time.

22
00:00:55,866 --> 00:00:56,766
One tile.

23
00:00:56,568 --> 00:00:57,886
Four more makes five.

24
00:00:57,886 --> 00:00:59,376
Nine more makes fourteen.

25
00:00:59,376 --> 00:01:00,908
Sixteen more makes thirty.

26
00:01:01,308 --> 00:01:02,221
Keep going.

27
00:01:02,221 --> 00:01:04,255
Fifty five, then ninety one.

28
00:01:04,655 --> 00:01:06,065
Is there a rule?

29
00:01:06,065 --> 00:01:10,413
Try n times n plus one, times
two n plus one, over six.

30
00:01:10,413 --> 00:01:12,544
It hits every total exactly.

31
00:01:13,194 --> 00:01:14,970
Why does that rule work?

32
00:01:14,970 --> 00:01:17,641
Here is a neat trick, in five steps.

33
00:01:18,041 --> 00:01:18,941
Step one.

34
00:01:18,918 --> 00:01:23,848
For any number k, k cubed minus k
minus one cubed works out to three k

35
00:01:23,848 --> 00:01:26,480
squared, minus three k, plus one.

36
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:28,130
Step two.

37
00:01:27,994 --> 00:01:32,229
Write this line for k equals one,
two, three, all the way to n.

38
00:01:32,229 --> 00:01:33,815
Then add all the lines.

39
00:01:34,565 --> 00:01:35,530
Step three.

40
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:38,445
On the left, the cubes cancel in pairs.

41
00:01:38,445 --> 00:01:40,113
Only n cubed survives.

42
00:01:40,863 --> 00:01:41,763
Step four.

43
00:01:41,732 --> 00:01:46,320
On the right, we get three times the
sum of squares, minus three times

44
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,079
the sum of k, plus n.

45
00:01:48,829 --> 00:01:49,729
Step five.

46
00:01:49,723 --> 00:01:52,170
Put in the sum of k, and solve.

47
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:57,434
The sum of squares is n times n plus
one, times two n plus one, over six.

48
00:01:58,184 --> 00:01:59,234
Now the cubes.

49
00:01:59,234 --> 00:02:01,613
One, plus eight, plus twenty seven.

50
00:02:01,613 --> 00:02:03,472
Watch what the totals become.

51
00:02:03,872 --> 00:02:04,772
One.

52
00:02:04,313 --> 00:02:05,213
Nine.

53
00:02:04,798 --> 00:02:05,698
Thirty six.

54
00:02:05,593 --> 00:02:06,493
One hundred.

55
00:02:06,432 --> 00:02:08,344
Every total is a perfect square!

56
00:02:08,744 --> 00:02:12,017
And look at the sides:
one, three, six, ten.

57
00:02:12,017 --> 00:02:13,765
Those are the sums of k.

58
00:02:13,765 --> 00:02:16,913
So the sum of cubes is
the sum of k, squared.

59
00:02:17,513 --> 00:02:18,571
Side by side.

60
00:02:18,571 --> 00:02:23,344
The sum of squares has a six at the
bottom, and two n plus one on top.

61
00:02:23,744 --> 00:02:25,917
The sum of cubes is simpler.

62
00:02:25,917 --> 00:02:29,094
Just square n times n plus one, over two.

63
00:02:29,694 --> 00:02:30,594
Your turn.

64
00:02:30,506 --> 00:02:34,022
Is the sum of the first n
cubes always a perfect square?

65
00:02:34,022 --> 00:02:35,170
Pause and think.

66
00:02:38,370 --> 00:02:39,270
Yes.

67
00:02:38,901 --> 00:02:42,134
It is n times n plus
one over two, squared.

68
00:02:42,134 --> 00:02:46,179
And n times n plus one over
two is always a whole number.

69
00:02:46,829 --> 00:02:47,816
Remember this.

70
00:02:47,816 --> 00:02:52,177
Three sums, three formulas: for
k, for k squared, and for k cubed.

71
00:02:52,877 --> 00:02:54,568
Now, one worked example.

72
00:02:54,918 --> 00:02:57,581
Find the sum of the
first ten square numbers.

73
00:02:57,581 --> 00:02:59,834
One, plus four, up to one hundred.

74
00:03:00,334 --> 00:03:01,234
Step one.

75
00:03:01,052 --> 00:03:02,095
Here n is ten.

76
00:03:02,845 --> 00:03:03,745
Step two.

77
00:03:03,670 --> 00:03:04,606
Put it in.

78
00:03:04,606 --> 00:03:07,958
Ten, times eleven, times
twenty one, over six.

79
00:03:08,708 --> 00:03:09,608
Step three.

80
00:03:09,553 --> 00:03:12,060
Ten times eleven is one hundred and ten.

81
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:15,503
Times twenty one gives two
thousand three hundred and ten.

82
00:03:16,253 --> 00:03:17,153
Step four.

83
00:03:17,046 --> 00:03:18,075
Divide by six.

84
00:03:18,075 --> 00:03:20,607
The sum is three hundred and eighty five.

85
00:03:21,357 --> 00:03:22,988
And the first ten cubes?

86
00:03:22,988 --> 00:03:26,138
Fifty five squared, three
thousand and twenty five.

87
00:03:26,788 --> 00:03:28,249
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:03:28,599 --> 00:03:32,425
Many students think the sum of
squares is the sum of k, squared.

89
00:03:32,425 --> 00:03:33,697
Try n equals three.

90
00:03:34,097 --> 00:03:37,774
The sum of k, squared, is
six squared, thirty six.

91
00:03:37,774 --> 00:03:40,837
But one plus four plus
nine is only fourteen.

92
00:03:40,837 --> 00:03:44,252
Squaring the sum gives the
cubes, not the squares.

93
00:03:44,902 --> 00:03:46,358
Where is this asked?

94
00:03:46,358 --> 00:03:47,365
In JEE Main.

95
00:03:47,365 --> 00:03:51,890
In April twenty nineteen, a paper
asked for one, plus two times three,

96
00:03:51,890 --> 00:03:54,754
plus three times five, up to eleven terms.

97
00:03:55,354 --> 00:03:56,595
Exam level now.

98
00:03:56,595 --> 00:04:00,957
Find the sum of k times k plus
one, for k from one to ten.

99
00:04:00,957 --> 00:04:02,250
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:09,062
The answer is B.

101
00:04:09,062 --> 00:04:10,858
Four hundred and forty.

102
00:04:11,358 --> 00:04:12,726
Open the bracket.

103
00:04:12,726 --> 00:04:15,880
k times k plus one is k squared, plus k.

104
00:04:16,380 --> 00:04:18,184
So the sum splits in two.

105
00:04:18,184 --> 00:04:21,987
The squares up to ten add to
three hundred and eighty five.

106
00:04:21,987 --> 00:04:23,947
The sum of k is fifty five.

107
00:04:24,447 --> 00:04:26,683
Add them: four hundred and forty.

108
00:04:26,683 --> 00:04:28,200
Option A is the trap.

109
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:29,764
It forgets the plus k.

110
00:04:30,364 --> 00:04:31,904
Here is your recap.

111
00:04:31,904 --> 00:04:33,486
Screenshot this card.

112
00:04:36,686 --> 00:04:38,872
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

113
00:04:39,272 --> 00:04:40,172
One.

114
00:04:39,683 --> 00:04:41,383
What does the sigma sign mean?

115
00:04:42,283 --> 00:04:43,183
Two.

116
00:04:42,728 --> 00:04:46,179
Find one, plus four, plus
nine, up to the fifth square.

117
00:04:47,079 --> 00:04:47,979
Three.

118
00:04:47,594 --> 00:04:50,338
Why is the sum of cubes
always a perfect square?

119
00:04:51,438 --> 00:04:54,349
That completes Chapter
8, sequences and series.

120
00:04:54,349 --> 00:04:57,755
Every episode is in the PUC
Maths Class eleven playlist.
